The weather conditions are also having a major impact on the Annapurna Circuit, one of Nepal’s key trekking routes. Hundreds of trekkers have had to abandon their journeys, with many airlifted by helicopter from Manang and Mustang after roads and bridges were damaged, according to media reports.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-32144","index":0} People displaced by the deadly flash floods and mudslides eat their lunch as they sit inside a makeshift kitchen at a temporary shelter in Betrawati, Nuwakot district, on 9 September 2026 ( Reuters )

Trekkers who attempted to leave overland described having to cross multiple landslide sites, damaged trails and flooded areas. Some took three-four days to reach safety, while others spent nights stranded in forests.

“Even thunder would make us run and come out to see if anything was coming our way,” Nepali guide Tse Ring Doma told the BBC.

if (document.cookie.split(';').some(cookie => cookie.trim() === '__DEBUG__=true')) { console.log('Ad logs: "taboola-carousel-thumbnails", injectedAtParagraph: 6'); }

A landslide hit an area above Samagaun village in Manaslu region at around 3am local time on Sunday, following several days of heavy rain. The noise startled hundreds of trekkers, climbers and expedition workers staying in the village.

“We simply rushed out of our rooms, looking for a safe place. We did not know where to go,” Abiral Rai, an accredited mountain guide stranded in Samagaun with his client, told The Everest Chronicle .

