In the higher mountains, climbers reportedly moved to lower ground as avalanches and snow made conditions increasingly dangerous.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-32145","index":1} Kalpana Thapa Magar, 43, displaced by flash floods, sits next to her damaged house in Betrawati, Nuwakot district, on 9 September 2026 ( Reuters )

Maya Lamdan, 27, and Nadav Lamdan, 21, were among the travellers airlifted from Samagaun, a remote Himalayan village where landslides had blocked roads and disrupted power supplies.

“Already after a few days of being stuck in the village, we saw that a rain system was approaching, and we stayed there so that we would be somewhere safe rather than continuing the trek. But the last 24 hours were far beyond what we expected,” Maya Lamdan told Israeli news site Walla .

She said the group was asleep at about 3am when someone knocked on their door and warned them to get onto the roof because floodwaters were approaching.

The next morning, they learned that two landslides had hit the area nearby.

“All the locals started running from the village toward the hills with headlamps. There was real panic.”

Jake Stacy, a trekker from Suffolk, was stranded with his guide in Yak Kharka, in upper Manang, after heavy snowfall last week.

He decided not to continue towards Thorang La pass, the highest point on the circuit. “We made our own paths on mountain slopes,” he told the BBC after returning to base camp.

open image in gallery {"id":"trigger-autogallery-32146","index":2} Search and rescue team members, along with sniffer dogs, search for victims following the flash floods on the bank of the Trishuli river in Nuwakot on 30 September 2026 ( AFP/Getty )

Last week, local authorities had warned of possible flash floods in 49 of Nepal’s 77 districts, with more than half of the country facing a high risk of flooding.

Earlier, it was reported that power had been disrupted in Manang, Mustang, Myagdi and Baglung. The rising Rahuganga river had damaged infrastructure and transmission towers linked to a 40MW hydropower project, according to water resources and energy minister Biraj Bhakta Shrestha.

Shanti Mahat, a spokesperson for the National Disaster Risk Reduction and Management Authority, said that Rasuwa and Nuwakot, which were badly affected by floods in August, should remain “alert”, although they were not currently classified as high-risk areas.

A landslide near Chame partially blocked a river, creating a temporary lake that flooded homes and a bridge. Authorities were trying to drain the accumulated water.

{"data":{"url":"https://twitter.com/WMO/status/2105291143231541383","isConversation":false,"align":"center"}}

Meanwhile, at least four people died and 12 remained missing after an avalanche near Himlung Peak, close to the Tibet border.

A rescue operation to recover the missing was temporarily suspended due to bad weather conditions.

The avalanche swept away the base camp of the Himlung Himal, a 7,126m peak in the Nar Phu area of Manang, on Sunday.

The latest spate of landslides and flash floods has affected tourism in the country. Nearly 700 trekkers have reportedly been waiting in Pokhara because authorities haven’t reopened the Annapurna Circuit.

Pilgrims travelling to Muktinath, a major Hindu pilgrimage site, have been advised to fly from Jomsom to Pokhara because roads are cut off.

A series of deadly events have made the past three months particularly dangerous for the climbing community.

“Mountaineering is inherently high risk at every step. The most important thing climbers can do is to hire experienced, qualified manpower, even if it costs more,” Tul Singh Gurung, chief of the National Mountain Guides Association, told Outside magazine.

“This is very important. The mountains will always be there, but if we make poor decisions, our lives won’t.”
